Subject: Object.assign must not use the object-spread fast path

What this does
  Routes Object.assign through assignment semantics rather than sharing the
  shape walk used by object spread.

Why we need it
  The spread walk defines properties, while assignment runs a setter on the target,
  refuses a read-only target in strict mode, and orders the copy as the assignment
  operation does.

What it adds
  Object.assign uses the assignment operation for each source property, so a
  setter runs, a read-only target throws as it should, and property order and
  enumerability are those of the assignment. Object literals with a spread keep the
  shape walk they already had. No opcode is added or retired and BC_VERSION is
  unchanged.

Needs a bytecode version bump
  No.

--- a/quickjs.c
+++ b/quickjs.c
@@ -19662,11 +19662,11 @@ static __exception int JS_CopyDataProperties(JSContext *ctx,
         return ret;
     }
 
-    /* No exclusion list means plain `{...src}` / Object.assign, which is the
-       shape worth specialising. `setprop` (Object.assign) would additionally
-       have to honour setters on the target; the fast path only accepts targets
-       that cannot have any, so both callers can share it. */
-    if (JS_VALUE_GET_TAG(excluded) != JS_TAG_OBJECT) {
+    /* Plain `{...src}` only, never Object.assign. The fast path defines
+       properties (CreateDataPropertyOrThrow, what spread requires);
+       Object.assign uses Set, which calls setters on the target and its
+       prototype chain and keeps existing attributes. */
+    if (!setprop && JS_VALUE_GET_TAG(excluded) != JS_TAG_OBJECT) {
         ret = JS_CopyDataPropertiesFast(ctx, target, source);
         if (ret != 0)
             return ret < 0 ? -1 : 0;
